sockets: no packet sockets or BPF filters without Linux's headers

The sockets extension includes linux/if_packet.h and linux/filter.h
where it finds them, and keeps the code for AF_PACKET sockets and for
SO_ATTACH_REUSEPORT_CBPF in any case, for every system whose headers
name them. Radix's C library names both, as Linux's does, and has
neither header: the kernel has no packet sockets and no BPF filters.
Without the headers, the names are taken out, as SO_BPF_EXTENSIONS and
SO_MEMINFO already are, and the extension builds without them.

diff --git a/ext/sockets/sockets.c b/ext/sockets/sockets.c
index e104126d..6c6452fc 100644
--- a/ext/sockets/sockets.c
+++ b/ext/sockets/sockets.c
@@ -63,9 +63,12 @@
 #  include <linux/filter.h>
 # else
 #  undef SO_BPF_EXTENSIONS
+#  undef SO_ATTACH_REUSEPORT_CBPF
 # endif
 # if defined(HAVE_LINUX_IF_PACKET_H)
 #  include <linux/if_packet.h>
+# else
+#  undef AF_PACKET
 # endif
 # if defined(HAVE_LINUX_IF_ETHER_H)
 #  include <linux/if_ether.h>
